ZSNES 2 Debian packaging notes
==============================

Package identity and coexistence
--------------------------------

The Debian source and binary package names are intentionally `zsnes2`, not
`zsnes`. ZSNES 2 is packaged as a separate maintained continuation and must be
co-installable with the historical `zsnes` package.

The package therefore does not declare `Provides`, `Replaces`, `Breaks`, or
`Conflicts` against `zsnes`. It installs the executable as `/usr/bin/zsnes2`,
uses `zsnes2`-specific desktop, AppStream, icon, and manual-page names, and
builds with `ZCONF="zsnes2"`. The latter prevents ZSNES 2 from adopting the
legacy `~/.zsnes` directory and gives it its own XDG configuration directory,
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zsnes2` (normally `~/.config/zsnes2`).

Architecture
------------

Upstream ZSNES 2.2.3 intentionally builds the Linux target as 32-bit x86
(`-m32`). The remaining assembly implementation is 32-bit x86 code, so the
binary package is correctly declared as Architecture: i386.

Do not relabel the package as amd64 merely because an amd64 system can run the
resulting ELF32 executable. amd64 users should install the i386 package through
Debian multiarch instead; this lets dpkg and apt resolve the matching 32-bit
runtime libraries correctly.

SDL3 and audio
--------------

Debian unstable provides `libsdl3-dev`, which is declared directly in
Build-Depends. No external repository is required to build the Debian package.

The package disables upstream's optional PipeWire and libao autodetection at
build time and uses SDL3 as the deterministic audio backend. This avoids build
results changing according to whichever optional development package happens
to be installed in the build environment.

Rendering
---------

OpenGL support remains enabled, but the SDL software renderer is also built.
A discrete GPU is therefore not a package requirement.
